"""The self-test of this enforcement point, against the counting Stripe double.

    AUTHS_SELFTEST_KEY_FILE=KEY_FILE bin/selftest

Run it once, on a fresh project, after ``bin/setup``, ``bin/install-gateway
< KEY_FILE``, and ``bin/start``. It sends every refund through the project's
own commands and through a witness: a relay on the application socket that
runs as its own process and records each exchange. It then checks:

- every hostile refund was decided by the gateway: the command's record says
  ``decided_by: "gateway"`` with the expected outcome and code, and the
  witness saw exactly one submission for it, answered with that result; a
  request refused only by ``request --precheck`` must fail that check, and
  the double saw exactly two refund writes;
- ``bin/audit`` fails the exported bundle by default, because the gateway
  signed no outcome for the refusals it made before recording anything, and
  passes it under ``--allow-unverified-refusals`` with the same report;
- eight tampered copies of the bundle fail ``bin/audit``, each run under
  ``--allow-unverified-refusals`` so that only the tampering can fail it; a
  ninth, an entry whose outcome was removed and whose proof was then altered,
  passes as the documented limit of that option;
- manager A, given an agent grant, submits a refund that managers A and B
  approve; the SDK refuses to author it, so it is the one refund built
  outside the project's commands, as a hostile client would, with the
  operator's and manager A's development keys. The gateway counts only B;
- a second ``bin/install-gateway`` is refused as already installed;
- the key in ``AUTHS_SELFTEST_KEY_FILE``, read only for this scan, appears
  in no file outside ``enforcement/gateway/``: none in the project, and none
  in ``$TMPDIR`` or ``/tmp`` written during the test.

It writes ``audit/selftest.json`` and prints the same report.
"""

